\begin{abstract}
Running a wireless learning experiment still means hand-assembling everything
around the algorithm: a radio stack, the wiring of every node, a testbed's
containers, and the ports between them. We present UnionLabs, a platform that
turns each of these into a designed, reusable part. An algorithm declares only
\emph{what to transmit} and \emph{what to receive}, and moves unchanged
between an ideal channel, a real single-carrier/OFDM modem on USRP hardware,
and LoRa. The wiring of a multi-node experiment is a declarative file in which
every link carries its own medium, per direction---checked when it is read,
not when it fails on the air. A shared workspace carries one account's
experiment state across testbeds that cannot reach each other, and an agent
turns any machine into a managed edge node, exposing hardware to experiment
containers only by the lab owner's permission. In this demonstration, visitors
run federated and decentralized learning unchanged across a laptop, a pair of
USRPs, and a shared testbed, rewiring the running experiment by editing one
field.
\end{abstract}

