%% ===========================================================================
\section{Motivation}
%% ===========================================================================
An experiment that places learning inside the radio loop---federated averaging
over the air~\cite{mcmahan2017fedavg}, decentralized consensus over a
graph~\cite{lian2017dpsgd}---is easy to state and expensive to run. The
algorithm is a page of code; what surrounds it is not. Shared platforms have
removed the hardware barrier~\cite{powder} and open physical-layer stacks the
waveform barrier~\cite{gnuradio}, but an experiment's \emph{wiring} still
lives in operators' heads and shell history: it is retyped on every machine
that takes part, and the retypings disagree---a client dials one port while
the server serves another, or a node is told to transmit on a radio that is
receive-only. The failure appears minutes later, from a layer that is not the
one at fault.

We introduce UnionLabs, a platform that separates \emph{what an algorithm
does} from \emph{how the experiment is wired}, and makes the second a
checkable artifact. \emph{The medium of every link---over the air, over
TCP/IP, or over LoRa---is a property of the link itself, declared per
direction, so the same unmodified algorithm runs on a laptop, on a pair of
radios, and across a shared testbed.} A wiring that cannot run is refused when
it is read, naming the node at fault, rather than after minutes of radio time.

%% ===========================================================================
\section{UnionLabs Design}
%% ===========================================================================
The new features of UnionLabs comprise four parts, each described below with
its own figure.

\textbf{Algorithm Contract.} An algorithm implements two functions,
\code{transmit()} and \code{receive(msg)}, plus an optional
\code{on\_result(ack)}, and declares the shape of its payload; it imports
nothing from any radio driver (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). The union layer
validates each payload, converts it between \code{ndarray} and bytes, and
maps the algorithm's own role names---client, server, peer---onto the ends of
a link. That one rule makes an experiment portable: the same unmodified file
runs the whole network in one process for development, splits into one
process per node for deployment, and moves between the ideal channel, the
USRP modem, and LoRa by changing one flag. A single self-test enforces the
claim over every radio-free physical layer before any radio is touched.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{fig-union-api-col}
\caption{The union API. A uniform path linking the user's algorithm to the
embedded wireless system.}
\Description{Four stacked blocks connected by double arrows: the experiment
file, the union contract, the transport, and the PHY.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}

\textbf{PHY Codes.} Beneath the contract is a C++ modem assembled from parts
we choose (Figure~\ref{fig:phy}): synchronization, carrier and phase
correction, equalization, BPSK through 16-QAM with differential variants,
convolutional, LDPC and turbo coding, CRC-16, and stop-and-wait ARQ, over
single-carrier or OFDM waveforms on USRP radios. Every choice is therefore an
experiment variable rather than a constant---modulation, coding, waveform,
gains, and the carrier are all knobs---and explicit ARQ makes the airtime a
learning round costs measured rather than modeled. The acknowledgement may
return over TCP or a second RF path, which lets a receive-only radio hold up
one end of a link; switching to OFDM replaces only the middle of the chain.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{fig-phy-pipeline-col}
\caption{The PHY design, with stop-and-wait ARQ applied.}
\Description{Transmit chain of framing, coding and modulation on the left;
receive chain of synchronization, equalization and decoding on the right; an
ACK arrow closes the loop.}
\label{fig:phy}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Wiring as a File.} A topology is a declarative document naming each
node, its role in the algorithm's own vocabulary, the radio it owns, the
ports it listens on, and the links between nodes; every node is launched with
the same file and its own identity, and a typed flag still wins. Each link
states how it is carried, and its two directions may differ---a rig with one
transmit-capable and one receive-only radio has exactly one RF path, so the
reply must return over TCP/IP, and a chain may change medium half way
through. A wiring that cannot run is refused when it is read, naming the node
at fault; one command walks 44 command-line flags and 82 topology settings
from the text a user types to the object each configures.

\textbf{Deployment on Shared Testbeds.} The platform image follows the
testbed's container contract; a session hook materializes the shared
workspace, publishes what the node is---its radios, addresses, and physical
layers---as a heartbeated record, and attaches the radio to the session's
namespace (Figure~\ref{fig:workspace}). The workspace holds two kinds of
state with two lifecycles: authored wiring, written once and valid until the
experimenter changes it, and discovered records, republished on a heartbeat
and expired with their node. That split is what lets one account's wiring
drive testbeds that cannot reach each other---the mounted file system is the
only thing they share, and it never carries a fact that can go stale.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{fig-shared-workspace-col}
\caption{The shared workspace across sessions under a single account.}
\Description{A central workspace box linked to four session boxes on
different testbeds; wiring flows to each session and records flow back.}
\label{fig:workspace}
\end{figure}

\textbf{The UnionLabs Agent.} One agent turns a lab owner's machine---server,
workstation, or laptop---into a managed edge node (Figure~\ref{fig:agent}).
It prepares the environment (AWS configuration, Docker, an RKE2 Kubernetes
server, Helm), registers the node under a unique edge ID, and speaks gRPC
with the cloud controller: instructions flow down, status and heartbeats flow
up. The owner uploads the testbed's topology through the visualizer, and the
reservation engine allocates from exactly that description---only nodes that
are provably alive are offered. When an experiment is scheduled, the agent
renders it into a Kubernetes deployment, mounts the shared EFS workspace, and
exposes only the permitted devices---Ethernet, USB radios, GPUs---into the
container. Remote access rides the same cloud--edge path, so pods are never
publicly exposed and the experimenter never configures a device path by hand.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{fig-agent-col}
\caption{The UnionLabs Agent: middleware between the cloud services and the
physical testbed.}
\Description{The cloud on top, the agent in the middle connected by a gRPC
channel, and below it the experiment pod and the edge devices it exposes by
permission.}
\label{fig:agent}
\end{figure}

%% ===========================================================================
\section{Demonstration}
%% ===========================================================================
We run the \emph{same} experiment file under three wirings, using a laptop and a
pair of USRPs at the table plus a live session on a remote testbed. Each
scenario takes two to three minutes, and visitors drive the wiring themselves.

\textbf{The Same Algorithm, With and Without Radios.} Federated averaging on
MNIST runs as three processes on one laptop, wired over TCP/IP; accuracy climbs
per round with no radio present. We then hand the visitor a second file and the
identical algorithm transmits each model over a 915\,MHz link from a B210 to an
N210, which answers over TCP/IP. Visitors watch acknowledgements,
retransmissions, and the airtime each round costs---the quantity that makes
communication a measured variable rather than a modeled one.

\textbf{A Chain That Changes Medium.} A three-node chain whose first hop is over
the air and whose second is Ethernet. The relay reports each leg it opens, and a
visitor can move the second hop back onto the radio by editing one field and
restarting a single node.

\textbf{Remote Testbed, and Deliberate Failure.} The same commands run on a
shared, Kubernetes-managed testbed with a network-attached radio and published
ports, so a wiring travels unchanged between machines we own and machines we do
not. Finally, a visitor asks a receive-only radio to transmit: the run refuses
at load, naming the node and link at fault.

